<h2>AI 2040: Plan A — the year-by-year scenario (2027 → 2040)</h2>
<p>This installment follows on from the <a href="#!news-20260709-ai-2040-plan-a">AI 2040: Plan A overview</a> and lays out the world the scenario paints <strong>year by year</strong>. Keep in mind that Plan A is not a prediction but a <strong>recommendation</strong> — it is a story about &ldquo;how things ought to go,&rdquo; not &ldquo;how things will go.&rdquo; What follows is the flow as cross-checked against published summaries and reporting, and the detailed figures follow the model in the original.</p>

<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">Before you read</span>
    The scenario starts from the premise that &ldquo;without intervention now, <strong>superintelligence arrives in 2030</strong>.&rdquo; All of Plan A&rsquo;s machinery is focused on <strong>pushing that moment back ten years</strong> to buy time to put safety infrastructure in place. The details of the supporting machinery are in the <a href="#!news-20260709-ai-2040-policy">policy and verification</a> installment.
</div>

<h3>2027 — two workforces</h3>
<p>At the scenario&rsquo;s starting point the United States has <strong>two workforces</strong>. One is <strong>165 million people</strong>; the other is <strong>AI agents</strong> — a population of which millions are created and terminated every hour, working ceaselessly at superhuman speed. Around this time (2027–2029) governments begin applying <strong>gating</strong> to frontier models.</p>

<h3>2028 — the election and data centers</h3>
<p>The 2028 election is heated as ever, and <strong>AI becomes the biggest issue</strong>. At this point the <strong>cost of the data centers under construction reaches twice the entire US defense budget</strong>. The same <strong>disruption of office work as a whole</strong> that software engineering went through in 2026 spreads, and many jobs turn into &ldquo;managing AI agents.&rdquo; AI companies industrialize the training process, automating occupations one after another: once executives decide &ldquo;this year let us go into [some particular profession],&rdquo; they interview experts, buy up data and build a training environment.</p>

<h3>2029 — the US–China agreement</h3>
<p>The United States and China <strong>agree to avoid a reckless superintelligence race</strong>. The two countries choose a <strong>Total Research Transparency</strong> approach as the high-level regulatory and transparency regime to aim for. This agreement is the branch point of the whole scenario.</p>

<h3>2030 — superintelligence averted</h3>
<p>On the original trajectory, this is the year <strong>AI R&amp;D would have been fully automated</strong>, reaching <strong>superintelligence</strong> by year&rsquo;s end. In Plan A this leap is <strong>deliberately averted</strong> thanks to the 2029 agreement.</p>

<h3>2030–2035 — expansion within the human range</h3>
<p>In this stretch AI grows gradually stronger <strong>within the range of human ability</strong>, reaching roughly <strong>top-human-expert</strong> systems. The key is to build capability without crossing into the superhuman domain.</p>

<h3>2035 — the pause</h3>
<p><strong>Development is paused</strong> at the expert level. The purpose is a single one — <strong>keeping humans in control</strong>. Time is allowed for safety and verification infrastructure to mature sufficiently before moving on to stronger systems.</p>

<h3>2040 — the release, and superintelligence</h3>
<p>On the premise that safety infrastructure is in place, <strong>the pause is lifted</strong> and capability is at last <strong>scaled up to superintelligence</strong>. This is why the name is &ldquo;AI 2040&rdquo; — because it is the scenario that <strong>pushed to 2040</strong> a superintelligence that could have come in 2030.</p>

